{"gid":"2026_04_NYJ_CHI","week":4,"away":"NYJ","home":"CHI","away_col":"#2FC98A","home_col":"#F1873B","spread":8.5,"total":45.5,"fav":"CHI","hpts":27,"apts":18,"kickoff":"Sun Oct 4 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Ground mismatch:</strong> CHI are run-first (40% pass when leading) into a NYJ front that bleeds <strong>132 rush yds/game</strong> and 0.62 RB1 rushing scores. <strong>D'Andre Swift</strong> is a centerpiece \u2014 and the game script only feeds him.</p><p>The <strong>CHI</strong> host the <strong>NYJ</strong> as favorites of <strong>8.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>45.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>CHI 27\u201318</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>39% of its snaps with CHI in front</strong>, <strong>35% within one score</strong>, and <strong>26% with CHI chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>CHI should control</strong>. A favorite of 8 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>CHI</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>NYJ</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>NYJ</strong> to run more total plays than CHI \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for NYJ's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>CHI are soft against the run (134 yds/game allowed), and NYJ feed <strong>Braelon Allen</strong> a heavy <strong>66%</strong> carry share \u2014 a rushing-yards spot.</p><p>CHI's outside-zone run game (31% to the edge) attacks a leaky NYJ front \u2014 chunk-run upside for <strong>D'Andre Swift</strong>.</p><p><strong>Geno Smith</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 CHI allow <strong>239 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Braelon Allen</strong> is a bell-cow (66% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.56 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Garrett Wilson</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 CHI surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone NYJ throw it (61% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Garrett Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Script leans CHI.</strong> As a 8-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>D'Andre Swift</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile NYJ throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Garrett Wilson</strong>.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>CHI \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>CHI are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 8-point favorite projected to lead for 39% of snaps, <strong>CHI</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>D'Andre Swift</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>20 carries for 90 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as CHI salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Luther Burden III</strong> (10 targets, 88 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when CHI lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Colston Loveland</strong> (TE, 7 targets, 55 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>NYJ \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>NYJ are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 NYJ project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Braelon Allen</strong>'s rushing projection (16 carries, 60 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Garrett Wilson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, NYJ funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>16 targets for 110 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Kenyon Sadiq</strong> (TE, 9 targets, 60 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game